Review record for the 0.2.1 patch release.

Five independent agents read 8bbdc00, the commit one step behind this
one. Four returned PASS: review-tests, review-code, review-deps,
review-usability. One returned FAIL, and the finding is fixed in
df9e859: the README, the CLI reference and the composition doc each
said `store sync` is the only command that reaches the network, and
`add`, `update` and `sync` fetch from a git remote too.

The driver verified df9e859 directly and signs off on that basis. Each
line below states what was run.

signoff[review-tests] PASS df9e859 carried the independent PASS at 8bbdc00, which ran the documented suite green at 115 cargo tests and 9 missouri paths with 178 assertions, and killed the crates.io published-version guard in the prepublish script by mutation; the change since is prose only, and the suite ran green again here; minor: the flake version derivation is read by no gate, and no test reads the README's install commands
signoff[review-code] PASS df9e859 carried the independent PASS at 8bbdc00, which matched the extracted tree against `git ls-tree`, evaluated the flake to `0.2.1` and `almanac`, and confirmed with `cargo metadata --locked --offline` that the lockfile agrees with the bumped manifest, which is the one mechanical failure mode a version bump has; the change since touches three markdown files and no code
signoff[review-docs] PASS df9e859 carried the independent FAIL at 8bbdc00 and fixed it: three documents claimed `store sync` is the only command reaching the network, while `add`, `update` and `sync` each fetch from a git remote, and restoring a missing entry on a fresh clone is the common path; SECURITY.md puts an undeclared fetch in scope, so a reader deciding whether almanac can run where egress is controlled got the wrong answer from all three; all three now name the four commands, and the composition doc says which to allow or refuse
